## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to frame a sign-assignment problem as a DP and implement both memoization and tabulation, a common interview pattern.

## Key Concepts
- DP state reduction to subset sum
- Space optimization with a 1D array
- Memoization vs bottom-up trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tabulation and Memo versions
function countTargetSumTab(nums, S) {
  const total = nums.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  if (Math.abs(S) > total || (total+S)%2 !== 0) return 0;
  const target = (total+S)/2;
  const dp = Array(target+1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const n of nums) {
    for (let t = target; t >= n; t--) dp[t] += dp[t-n];
  }
  return dp[target];
}

function countTargetSumMemo(nums, S) {
  const total = nums.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0);
  if (Math.abs(S) > total || (total+S)%2 !== 0) return 0;
  const target = (total+S)/2;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, sum){
    if (sum > target) return 0;
    if (i === nums.length) return sum === target ? 1 : 0;
    const key = i+':'+sum;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i+1, sum+nums[i]) + dfs(i+1, sum);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0,0);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine basic structures (arrays/deque, hashmap, heap) to maintain a sliding window’s frequency distribution and extract top-K on demand.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window maintenance with a deque
- Frequency counting via a hashmap
- Min-heap with lazy deletions for top-K
- Amortized update and top-K retrieval costs

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowTopK {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W
    this.window = [] // acts as deque (push is at end, shift from start)
    this.counts = new Map()
    this.heap = [] // min-heap of [freq, value]
  }
  _pushHeap(pair) { /* heap push for [freq, value] */ }
  add(x) {
    this.window.push(x)
    this.counts.set(x, (this.counts.get(x) || 0) + 1)
    // push updated freq
    this._pushHeap([this.counts.get(x), x])
    if (this.window.length > this.W) {
      const y = this.window.shift()
      const f = (this.counts.get(y) || 0) - 1
      if (f <= 0) this.counts.delete(y)
      else this.counts.set(y, f)
      this._pushHeap([f, y])
    }
  }
  topK(K) {
    const res = []
    const seen = new Set()
    const pulled = []
    while (res.length < Math.min(K, this.counts.size) && this.heap.length) {
      const [freq, val] = this._popHeap()
      if (!this.counts.has(val) || this.counts.get(val) !== freq) continue
      if (!seen.has(val)) {
        seen.add(val)
        res.push(val)
        pulled.push([freq, val])
      }
    }
    // restore elements for future calls
    for (const p of pulled) this._pushHeap(p)
    return res
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
